/// Bitstring — a sequence of bits that is not a whole number of bytes.
///
/// A BEAM bitstring whose bit size is a multiple of 8 is a binary, and has
/// class `Binary` (or `String`). Any other bitstring is a `Bitstring`. The
/// class follows the value's shape, as in Erlang, so an operation can turn
/// one into the other: joining 1 bit and 7 bits answers a `Binary`.
///
/// Bitstrings come from binary patterns: a `/bitstring` (or `/bits`)
/// segment binds whatever bits are left, and an unaligned tail is a
/// `Bitstring` (ADR 0141).
///
/// `Bitstring` is a sibling of `Binary`, not a superclass or subclass. It
/// understands only bit-level messages. The byte protocol of `Binary` and
/// `Collection` (`size`, `do:`, `at:`, `toBytes`, `asString`, …) is
/// deliberately not understood: a send raises `does_not_understand` with a
/// hint naming the bit size. Use `bitSize`/`toBits`, or `asBinary` to check
/// alignment.
///
/// ## BEAM Mapping
/// A Beamtalk Bitstring is an Erlang `bitstring()` that is not a `binary()`.
///
/// ## Examples
/// ```beamtalk
/// tail := (Binary fromBytes: #(234)) match: [<<_:3, rest/bitstring>> -> rest]
/// tail class          // => Bitstring
/// tail bitSize        // => 5
/// tail toBits         // => #(0, 1, 0, 1, 0)
/// tail printString    // => "<<10:5>>"
/// tail asBinary isError   // => true
/// ```
sealed typed Value subclass: Bitstring native: beamtalk_bitstring

  /// Return the number of bits.
  ///
  /// `Binary>>bitSize` answers the same for a byte-aligned value, so
  /// `bitSize` works on either class.
  ///
  /// ## Examples
  /// ```beamtalk
  /// tail bitSize   // => 5
  /// ```
  bitSize -> Integer => self delegate

  /// Return the bits as a List of 0/1 Integers, most significant bit first.
  ///
  /// ## Examples
  /// ```beamtalk
  /// tail toBits   // => #(0, 1, 0, 1, 0)
  /// ```
  toBits -> List(Integer) => self delegate

  /// Join this bitstring's bits with `other`'s.
  ///
  /// `other` may be a Bitstring, a Binary, or a String (joined by its UTF-8
  /// bytes, as `Binary>>concat:` does). The answer is a `Binary` when the
  /// joined bits are byte-aligned, and a `Bitstring` otherwise.
  ///
  /// ## Examples
  /// ```beamtalk
  /// (oneBit concat: sevenBits) class   // => Binary
  /// (oneBit concat: oneBit) class      // => Bitstring
  /// ```
  concat: other :: Bitstring | Binary -> Bitstring | Binary => self delegate

  /// Convert to a Binary, as a Result.
  ///
  /// A Bitstring is never byte-aligned, so this is always an error of kind
  /// `#unaligned_bitstring`. There is no silent padding or truncation.
  ///
  /// ## Examples
  /// ```beamtalk
  /// tail asBinary isError            // => true
  /// tail asBinary error kind         // => #unaligned_bitstring
  /// ```
  asBinary -> Result(Binary, Error) => self delegate

  /// Return the Erlang notation for the bits, e.g. `<<2:3>>`.
  ///
  /// ## Examples
  /// ```beamtalk
  /// tail printString   // => "<<10:5>>"
  /// ```
  printString -> String => self delegate

  /// Return the same Erlang notation as `printString`.
  ///
  /// ## Examples
  /// ```beamtalk
  /// tail displayString   // => "<<10:5>>"
  /// ```
  displayString -> String => self delegate
